      "worked": "The domain excludes $x=0$ and $x=-1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}4x-1&=k(x^2+x)\\\\kx^2+(k-4)x+1&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $k=0$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}-4x+1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis is one valid real solution.\n\nFor $k\\ne0$, the equation is quadratic. At most one real solution means zero or one real root, so $\\Delta\\le0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(k-4)^2-4k\\\\\\Delta&=k^2-12k+16\\\\\\Delta&=(k-6)^2-20.\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(k-6)^2&\\le20\\\\-2\\sqrt5&\\le k-6\\le2\\sqrt5\\\\6-2\\sqrt5&\\le k\\le6+2\\sqrt5.\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $P(x)=kx^2+(k-4)x+1$, $P(0)=1$ and $P(-1)=k-(k-4)+1=5$. Neither excluded denominator value can be a polynomial root, so no roots are lost through the domain restrictions.\n\nTherefore, $k=0$ or $6-2\\sqrt5\\le k\\le6+2\\sqrt5$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
